---
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { resolve } from "node:path";
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { codeTheme } from "../../components/code-theme";
import { noFlashScript } from "../../scripts/theme";
import { readTokens } from "../../utils/tokens";
// Read from the file the registry ships, so the table never drifts from the real values. A `?raw`
// CSS import would be empty under Vitest (`css: false`), so read it from the project root.
const tokens = readTokens(readFileSync(resolve("registry/styles/buril.css"), "utf8"));

const darkExample = `<html class="dark">  <!-- always dark -->
<html class="light"> <!-- always light -->
<html>               <!-- follows the system -->`;

const overrideExample = `:root {
  --decor: #1d4ed8;
  --ring: #1d4ed8;
}

.dark {
  --decor: #60a5fa;
  --ring: #60a5fa;
}

@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root:not(.light) {
    --decor: #60a5fa;
    --ring: #60a5fa;
  }
}`;

const code = "mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Theming | Buril UI"
  description="Buril's design tokens, dark mode and how to change the palette."
>
  <h1 class="font-display text-3xl">Theming</h1>
  <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted-foreground">
    Components read the standard shadcn tokens, so any shadcn theme works. The optional Buril theme
    sets them to ink on paper, with one red for focus, decoration and destructive actions.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Token layers</h2>
  <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>
      <strong>Primitives</strong>, <code>--buril-*</code>: the raw palette, such as
      <code>--buril-paper</code> and <code>--buril-ink</code>. They never change with the color
      scheme. Only this docs site uses them; the registry theme writes the final values.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>shadcn variables</strong>, such as <code>--background</code> and
      <code>--primary</code>. They switch between light and dark, and they are the only tokens
      components depend on.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Buril extras</strong>: <code>--decor</code> for decoration and <code>--grid</code> for
      grid lines. Decoration always has a fallback, so components still work without them.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p class="mt-4">
    An <code>@theme inline</code> block maps each variable to a Tailwind color, so
    <code>bg-background</code> and <code>text-primary</code> follow the active scheme.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Tokens</h2>
  {/* Values wrap anywhere, so the table fits a 320px screen without scrolling sideways. */}
  <div class="mt-4 border border-border">
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <caption id="tokens-caption" class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-muted-foreground">
        {/* An expression keeps the space: Prettier drops a plain one before <code> here. */}
        {"Buril values for each token, read from "}
        <code>registry/styles/buril.css</code>.
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-y border-border">
          <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-2 font-semibold">
            Token
          </th>
          <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-2 font-semibold">
            Light
          </th>
          <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-2 font-semibold">
            Dark
          </th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {tokens.map(({ name, light, dark }) => (
          <tr class="border-b border-border last:border-b-0">
            <th scope="row" class="px-4 py-2 font-normal wrap-anywhere">
              <code>--{name}</code>
            </th>
            {[light, dark].map((value) => (
              <td class="px-4 py-2">
                <span class="flex items-center gap-2">
                  <span
                    aria-hidden="true"
                    class="size-4 shrink-0 rounded-full border border-border"
                    style={`background: ${value}`}
                  />
                  <code class="min-w-0 wrap-anywhere">{value}</code>
                </span>
              </td>
            ))}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Dark mode</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">
    The theme reads two classes on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>: <code>.dark</code> turns dark mode on
    and <code>.light</code> forces light mode. With neither class, a
    <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> media query follows the system, so the page matches the user's
    setting without JavaScript.
  </p>
  <div class={code}>
    <Code code={darkExample} lang="html" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>
  <p class="mt-4">
    The theme redefines Tailwind's <code>dark:</code> variant with the same rule, so
    <code>dark:</code> utilities agree with the tokens. A toggle must therefore set
    <code>.light</code> to force light mode; removing <code>.dark</code> only returns to the system
    setting. With next-themes, <code>attribute="class"</code> already does this.
  </p>

  <h3 class="mt-8 font-display text-lg">Avoid a flash</h3>
  <p class="mt-4">
    If you store the visitor's choice, apply it before the first paint with an inline script at the
    top of <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>. This site runs this one:
  </p>
  <div class={code}>
    <Code code={noFlashScript} lang="js" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Override the red</h2>
  <p class="mt-4">
    The red carries meaning, so it is limited to three tokens: <code>--decor</code> for decoration,
    <code>--ring</code> for the focus ring and <code>--destructive</code> for destructive actions
    and errors. To use your own color, redefine them after the theme, in all three blocks:
  </p>
  <div class={code}>
    <Code code={overrideExample} lang="css" theme={codeTheme} />
  </div>
  <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6">
    <li>
      Keep <code>--ring</code> at least 3:1 against <code>--background</code>, so focus stays
      visible.
    </li>
    <li>
      Keep <code>--destructive</code> at least 4.5:1 against <code>--background</code>, because
      error text uses it.
    </li>
    <li>Measure both schemes: a color that passes on paper can fail on the dark background.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
